This leaves another question on the table though, how does one go from vulnerable @POS:a to centralized and hardened servers of
a global organization?

Well, you could brute force it for one - but that takes time and is prone to problems of its own right.

A simpler process would be to take a known variable - like say a credit card number.

Then, as you are monitoring local network traffic while you casually relax in the living room,
look for that credit card number in the billions of other @POS:a packets to find where it goes and trace the route.

This gives you valuable routing information for you to find a way back to the hardened centralized servers
and provide a path to load a malware payload onto a deployment server that blasts it directly to thousands of @POS:a devices
around the globe in just a few short weeks.

Then all you have to do is abscond with access credentials to deploy it.

In the context of the Hyatt hack, that doesn't seem too hard if they have to give elevated access to network
